# Line endings and how to apply (CORRECTED 2026-09-23 — an earlier version
# of this header wrongly claimed BOTH target files use CRLF; that was false
# and, combined with a single blanket `git apply` invocation, risks
# CORRUPTING CLAUDE.md, which is pure LF on disk, into CRLF on apply).
# Verified live against fresh copies of both real files, 2026-09-23:
#   - CLAUDE.md is PURE LF.
#   - skills/team-orchestration/SKILL.md is CRLF.
# This repo's own .gitattributes forces `*.patch text eol=lf`, so every hunk
# below is stored as plain LF regardless of which target it's against —
# that's not a shortcut, it's required (an embedded \r inside a tracked
# *.patch file's content is indistinguishable from a line ending to git's
# own normalization and gets silently stripped on `git add` here; verified
# 2026-09-23 by staging a byte-preserved CRLF-hunk draft of this file and
# reading back the blob with `git show :<path>` — 0 embedded \r survived).
# The correctness question is therefore entirely about HOW YOU APPLY an
# all-LF patch to two targets with different real line endings, not about
# what bytes this file stores. Verified 2026-09-23, byte-exact against
# hand-built expected copies of both real files (`diff -q`: no difference):
# a SINGLE `git apply --whitespace=fix -p1` invocation correctly
# reconstructs SKILL.md's real CRLF from this all-LF patch (git's own
# whitespace-fix path restores the target's existing line-ending
# convention) — but the SAME invocation, in the SAME run, also rewrites
# CLAUDE.md's genuine LF to CRLF wherever this operator's machine has
# `core.autocrlf=true` (confirmed set globally here), because that
# conversion happens at git's working-tree-write step for anything it
# treats as "text" and is orthogonal to what the patch itself contains.
# Apply as TWO invocations instead, one per target, from
# {{USER_CLAUDE_DIR}}:
#
#   git -c core.autocrlf=false apply --whitespace=fix -p1 --include=CLAUDE.md THIS_FILE
#   git apply --whitespace=fix -p1 --include='skills/team-orchestration/SKILL.md' THIS_FILE
#
# The first invocation's `-c core.autocrlf=false` is what keeps CLAUDE.md
# LF; the second needs the operator's normal (autocrlf-enabled) settings so
# `--whitespace=fix` performs the CRLF reconstruction SKILL.md needs — don't
# add `-c core.autocrlf=false` to the second invocation, and don't reach for
# `core.whitespace=cr-at-eol` as a shortcut to unify the two into one
# invocation (tried 2026-09-23: it made the SKILL.md hunk fail to match
# entirely on this git version, worse than the problem it was meant to
# solve).
#
# Hunks use a 1-line context window (not git's default 3) deliberately: the
# wider default pulled an unrelated, operator-specific paragraph two lines
# above the "Model routing" heading into the diff as unchanged context,
# which has no business being copied into this repo (see CONTRIBUTING's
# leak-check note) — 1 line of context is still enough for `git apply` to
# locate every hunk unambiguously against the real files.
#
# Source facts: plugins/agent-companion/config/model-tiers.json (this
# branch), live-fetched 2026-09-23 from platform.claude.com and
# code.claude.com — see that config's `aliasResolution` and per-tier
# `resolvesTo` blocks for citations, and its new `cacheTtl` block
