+Three checks, enforced by the `agent-companion` plugin (warn-only — a false block stops legitimate work more than a bad spawn costs):
 
-- **`fable` is an exception, not a tier.** It sits in the same alias namespace as `opus`/`sonnet`/`haiku`, so it is one word away at every spawn, but it belongs to no weight on the scale. Spawning it requires a stated warrant naming the weight and why a cheaper tier cannot do the job. This is enforced, not merely requested - see the `agent-companion` plugin.
+1. **Every spawn names a definition stating BOTH model and effort** (a ladder `ac-*` def or a project def). A spawn with no model, or a definition with no effort, inherits the lead's model and effort — that counts as a violation, not a safety margin. `hooks/spawn-guard.mjs` warns on either gap.
+2. **Build check before opus/fable-tier work.** If the SESSION's own Claude Code build is below `config/model-tiers.json`'s `aliasResolution.minClaudeCodeVersion`, restart the session before spawning — don't pin around it. Measured 2026-09-23: subagents from sessions on build 2.1.275 resolved `opus` to Opus 5, while sessions on 2.1.280 got Opus 5.5 — same alias, different model, no error either way. The desktop app bundles its own build separate from the `claude` CLI on PATH, and a session keeps the build it started with, so this is read from the SESSION's own transcript, never from `claude --version`.
+3. **Verify the resolved model.** The model a spawn actually ran on (from its subagent transcript) must match what its definition's alias resolves to on that build. `scripts/audit.mjs`'s `model-resolution-mismatch` check scans recent history (bounded to ~48h) and flags mismatches with the session build that produced them.
