+- **Cost levers are tier + effort + prompt caching.** `effort` scales ALL output (thinking + answer + tool calls) — see the plugin's per-alias `resolvesTo.defaultEffort` and effort-support list for what each current model actually accepts (older pinned versions differ: e.g. Opus 4.6 and Sonnet 4.6 have no `xhigh`). `max` adds significant cost for relatively small quality gains - reserve it for genuinely frontier problems. Use `low` for subagents doing simple work.
 - **Delegation is cheap only if the WORKER is cheap.** Every fresh subagent spawn pays a cold cache write; subagents were measured at 38.9% of a 30-day spend. The old line "over-delegation just wastes a haiku agent" holds ONLY when the agent is actually haiku. Under model inheritance, over-delegation is expensive - which is why the explicit-model rule above matters more than the delegate-everything rule.
-- **Caching is already solved; do not spend effort there.** Measured hit ratio 95.9% overall, uniform across models. The remaining levers are tier mix and turn volume, not prompt-prefix tuning.
+- **Caching's hit ratio is high; the misses are what cost money.** Under 5 minutes, 99.8% hit. Past it, subagents (5m TTL by default, regardless of subscription) rewrite instead of reading — 5–60 minute gaps produced 35.7% of all subagent cache-write tokens over 30 days (measured 2026-09-23, `~/.claude/reports/2026-09-23-subagent-cache-ttl.md`). See the resume-window rule below and the plugin's `config/model-tiers.json` `cacheTtl` block before assuming caching needs no attention.
 - **On a Max subscription the currency is the usage allowance, not dollars.** Same levers; the goal is to stretch the allowance and avoid throttling. Size the lead to the session, recover the lead's cost via delegation depth + worker effort (not by downgrading the decision-maker), default Opus agents to `xhigh` not `max`, leave fast mode off unless speed is needed NOW, and lean on cheaper tiers for reads/searches/reviews.
